  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-8y+11=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)\\,$",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-8y+11 & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\cdot 0-8y+11 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-8y+11 & =0 \\\\ [{y}^{2}-8y]+11 & =0 \\\\ [(y-4)^{2}-16]+11 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2}-5 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =5 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA\\,(0,4+\\sqrt{5})\\,\\text{and}\\,B\\,(0,4-\\sqrt{5}).\n$$",
